00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  at the turn of the 21st century  scientists predicted that by 2020  climate change will have increased the  number of people at risk of malaria  by 60 percent other diseases  such as mosquito-borne yellow fever and  tick-borne encephalitis  have been projected to escalate in  transmission in a warmer world  too these hypotheses were grounded in  the understanding that major vectors for  infectious diseases  like mosquitoes and ticks experience  population increases  in warmer weather over the past several  decades our planet has undoubtedly  warmed  but the trend in infectious disease  transmission has not been as clear  so what drives the transmission of  infectious diseases  and what does climate change have to do  with it  let's start by understanding what  infectious diseases  are and how they spread to humans  some of the most common and devastating  infectious diseases  include malaria west nile virus  ebola dengue yellow fever  and zika pathogens are the  micro-organisms that cause infectious  diseases  and they come in a variety of shapes and  sizes  from sub-microscopic viruses to  unicellular bacteria and protozoa  to multicellular parasites  pathogens can be divided into two broad  categories  based on how they transmit diseases to  humans  first there are anthroponoses which have  evolved to use  humans as their primary hosts  then there are zoanices which use  non-human animals  as their hosts zoanices can be  transmitted to humans directly  such as a bite from a rabbit animal or  indirectly through intermediate hosts  called  vectors vector-borne zoanises  play a key role in how climate may  influence the spread of infectious  diseases  now we can begin to answer the question  how might climate change  exacerbate infectious disease  transmission  one major aspect is the biological  response to warming  pathogens vectors and hosts each have a  range of temperatures  within which they can survive this can  be represented  as a thermal performance curve with an  organism's biological function  rising from a lower limit peaking at an  optimal temperature  and then falling at an upper limit  species with a narrow thermal  performance curve  are more vulnerable to temperature  changes while those with broad curves  are more resilient in addition to  intrinsic thermal limits  how an organism might respond to a  warmer world  also depends on its ability to regulate  its own body temperatures  shift major life cycle events such as  the timing of reproduction  or migrate to places with an optimal  climate  as higher latitudes and altitudes get  warmer  pathogen carrying vectors that are able  to  will migrate poleward or higher in  elevation  this could expose native hosts to a  disease which they have never  encountered before  and therefore have no acquired immune  response  that would help protect them aside from  these biological factors  disease transmission can also be  affected by economics  and other human actions on the one hand  human behavior could mask the effects of  climate  on an infectious disease for example  a region that has become warmer and  wetter with a climate change would in  theory  see an increase in mosquito populations  and therefore  an increase in malaria transmission  but if that region implements more  robust healthcare  or adopts preventative measures like  insecticide treated bed nets  malaria transmission might stay the same  or even  go down this explains why many wealthier  countries  despite having climates suitable for  mosquitoes have practically eradicated  malaria transmission  while less wealthy countries with  similar climates  still grapple with the disease on the  other hand  the combination of climate change  economics and human behavior  could amplify an infectious disease  for instance if climate change causes  crop failures  or livestock deaths due to floods  droughts or heat waves  people may be driven to eat or sell wild  animals  in order to alleviate food insecurity or  economic  hardship in fact scientists have  inferred  that some of the past ebola outbreaks  were caused by the hunting  and consumption of wild chimpanzees and  fruit bats  as we have learned the biological  relationship between climate change and  infectious disease  is incredibly complex and it is  complicated  by external factors and human behaviors  that may  mask or amplify the effects of a rapidly  changing climate  ecologists and epidemiologists are hard  at work trying to better understand  these questions  in the meantime don't forget your bug  repellent  [Music]  you

